########################################################################
# PRAVAAH · M5 — Micro-Insurance (usage-based cover · reputation-priced · claims)
#
# VS Code "REST Client" / JetBrains HTTP client.  Base = /api/v1/...
#
# M4 proved the platform can move money. M5 proves the reputation is worth
# more than one product: the SAME consented behaviour (M1) and the SAME
# explainable score (M2) price micro-insurance for a population insurers
# price blindly or refuse — then collect premium and pay claims over the
# SAME double-entry ledger.
#
# ─── READ THIS BEFORE SENDING ANYTHING ────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. PRICING IS PERSONAL, SO THE MARKETPLACE SHOWS NO PRICES.
#    `GET /insurance/products` returns cover bands and terms, never a
#    premium. A premium only exists once somebody has been underwritten,
#    because the whole point is that two applicants pay different amounts
#    for identical cover. §10 is where the number appears.
#
# 2. A POLICY CAN ONLY BE BOUGHT FROM A QUOTE.
#    Same discipline as M4's FX quote, different reason: an FX quote freezes
#    a market rate, an insurance quote freezes an UNDERWRITING DECISION.
#    Between quoting and buying, a score can be recomputed or an admin can
#    reprice the product — and the person must be sold the cover they were
#    shown, at the price they were shown, on the evidence that produced it.
#
# 3. AMOUNTS ARE INTEGER MINOR-UNIT STRINGS.
#    `"2000000"` is ₹20,000.00 — paise, never rupees. Sent as strings
#    because `JSON.parse` turns any numeric literal into a float before a
#    line of our code runs.
#
# 4. BUYING NEEDS AN `Idempotency-Key`.
#    A retried purchase returns the SAME policy, never a second debit.
#
# 5. THE PREMIUM COMES FROM THE PRAVAAH WALLET.
#    Not from a card and not from a fresh UPI collect. So §20 will FAIL with
#    a shortfall until the wallet has money — run the M4 topup (§05 of
#    m4-payments.http) first. That failure is the honest path, not a bug:
#    cover is bound only once a premium has actually SETTLED on the ledger.
#
# ─── PREREQUISITES ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   • `npm run prisma:push && npm run prisma:seed` — the seed creates the
#     three products (Income Protection, Health Top-up, Travel & Accident).
#   • A VERIFIED user (M0) with an ACTIVE AA consent (M1) and a COMPUTED
#     score (M2). Without a score you are still quoted — see §11, the
#     thin-file path — but the reputation contrast needs one.
#   • Wallet balance in the product's currency (M4 §05 + §06).
#
# ─── THE SHOWPIECE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   §11  the SAME cover quoted to two different bands. That contrast — one
#        applicant paying ~₹97/mo and another ~₹205/mo for identical
#        ₹20,000 cover — is the entire product in one screenshot.
#   §41  a FORGED document. `{"tamper": true}` scores 0.92 and the claim is
#        hard-routed to a human instead of auto-paid, no matter how small.
#   §62  the loss ratio, read off the LEDGER rather than from application
#        tables — so it reconciles against M4's trial balance (§90 there).
#
#   No-database proof of all five claims:  npm run verify:insurance
########################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8000/api/v1
@token = PASTE_USER_ACCESS_TOKEN
@adminToken = PASTE_ADMIN_ACCESS_TOKEN

# The seeded product ids. Fixed UUIDs, so these are stable across re-seeds.
@incomeProtectionId = 8f3a1c20-0000-4000-8000-000000000001
@healthTopupId = 8f3a1c20-0000-4000-8000-000000000002
@travelAccidentId = 8f3a1c20-0000-4000-8000-000000000003

# Filled in as you go.
@quoteId = PASTE_QUOTE_ID
@policyId = PASTE_POLICY_ID
@claimId = PASTE_CLAIM_ID
@docId = PASTE_CLAIM_DOCUMENT_ID


########################################################################
# 01 · THE MARKETPLACE
########################################################################

### 01 — every active product. Cover bands and terms, deliberately no prices.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/products
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 02 — filter to one currency.
# A Singapore-resident holder paying premium from an SGD wallet must not be
# offered an INR policy that would then pay a claim in a currency they cannot
# spend where they are.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/products?currency=SGD
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 03 — one product, with its exclusions in plain words and its disclaimer.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/products/{{incomeProtectionId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}


########################################################################
# 10 · THE QUOTE — the reputation moment
########################################################################

### 10 — ask for a price on ₹20,000 of income protection.
#
# The response carries, in one payload:
#   • `premium`            — what you pay, per period
#   • `standardPremium`    — what a STANDARD-risk applicant pays for the
#                            same cover
#   • `savingsPercent`     — the difference, which IS the product's pitch
#   • `riskTier` + `explanation[]` — why, in sentences a person can contest
#   • `waitingPeriodDays`  — extended for a SUBSTANDARD risk
#   • `exclusions[]` + `disclaimer` — the non-advice stance, on every priced
#                            response rather than on a screen that might
#                            forget it
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{incomeProtectionId}}",
  "coverageMinor": "2000000"
}

### 11 — THE SHOWPIECE: the same request, from a different band.
#
# Run §10 as the seeded VERY_GOOD user, then again as the FAIR user. Identical
# `coverageMinor`, materially different `premium`, and `explanation[]` says why
# in both cases. That contrast is what an insurer judge is looking for, because
# it is the thing no insurer can currently do for this population.
#
# `npm run verify:insurance` proves the same four bands separate, with no
# database involved.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{incomeProtectionId}}",
  "coverageMinor": "2000000"
}

### 12 — a POOR band is DECLINED, politely, with a route back.
#
# 200, not 4xx. The decline is a recorded quote with `riskTier: "DECLINE"`, a
# `declineReason` in plain language, and a pointer to M2's improvement tips —
# because "not eligible" with no reason is the experience this population
# already gets everywhere, and repeating it while holding their consented data
# would be worse than not offering the product.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{incomeProtectionId}}",
  "coverageMinor": "2000000"
}

### 13 — cover outside the product's band → 400, with the band in the message.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{incomeProtectionId}}",
  "coverageMinor": "99999999"
}

### 14 — a SUBSTANDARD applicant asking for the maximum hits the TIER ceiling.
# The one adverse-selection control whose input the applicant chooses, which is
# exactly why the cap is applied server-side and the reduced ceiling is stated
# rather than the request silently clamped.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{incomeProtectionId}}",
  "coverageMinor": "5000000"
}

### 15 — read a quote back. Note `validUntil` — 30 minutes.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/quotes/{{quoteId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 16 — a TRAVEL quote. One-time premium, pro-rated to a 30-day term.
# Not a full year's premium on a fortnight of cover: accident cover is priced
# per day of exposure.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/quote
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "productId": "{{travelAccidentId}}",
  "coverageMinor": "100000"
}


########################################################################
# 20 · BUY — premium on the live M4 ledger
########################################################################

### 20 — buy the cover. Change the key when you mean a NEW purchase.
#
# `expectedPremiumMinor` is a cross-check, not an instruction: the server never
# charges this number, it refuses to proceed when the quote disagrees. A client
# showing a stale screen is rejected rather than silently charged the
# correct-but-unseen figure — which matters more here than on a transfer,
# because this is a figure the holder will be debited every month.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Idempotency-Key: buy-policy-001
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "expectedPremiumMinor": "15000",
  "acceptedDisclaimer": true
}

### 21 — REPLAY. Same key, same body → the SAME policy, no second debit.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Idempotency-Key: buy-policy-001
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "expectedPremiumMinor": "15000",
  "acceptedDisclaimer": true
}

### 22 — a stale premium → 409. The client was showing an old screen.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Idempotency-Key: buy-policy-stale-001
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "expectedPremiumMinor": "9999",
  "acceptedDisclaimer": true
}

### 23 — the disclaimer cannot be defaulted through → 400.
# `acceptedDisclaimer` is `z.literal(true)` with no default. An insurance
# purchase where the non-advice disclaimer could be skipped is a purchase
# nobody acknowledged.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Idempotency-Key: buy-policy-nodisc-001
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "expectedPremiumMinor": "15000"
}

### 24 — an EMPTY WALLET leaves the policy PENDING_PAYMENT, not ACTIVE.
#
# The honest path, and the most important assertion in this file: cover is bound
# only once the premium has SETTLED on the ledger. The response carries the
# shortfall so the app can say how much to add. An implementation that activated
# on intent would be insuring people who never paid — and would discover it at
# claim time, which is the worst possible moment for both sides.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Idempotency-Key: buy-policy-broke-001
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "quoteId": "{{quoteId}}",
  "expectedPremiumMinor": "15000",
  "acceptedDisclaimer": true
}

### 25 — top the wallet up (M4 §05/§06), then retry the FIRST premium.
#
# Its own route rather than a repeat of §20: the policy already exists at the
# agreed price, so re-buying would send the holder back through underwriting at
# a price that may have moved. Nobody should be repriced for having had an empty
# wallet on the day they decided to buy cover.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies/{{policyId}}/retry-premium
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Idempotency-Key: retry-premium-001
Content-Type: application/json

{}


########################################################################
# 30 · THE POLICY WALLET
########################################################################

### 30 — my policies. Lapsed and expired ones INCLUDED by default.
# Hiding a lapsed policy is how somebody discovers they were uninsured at claim
# time. `activeOnly=true` is available and is not the default, on purpose.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 31 — one policy, with its waiting-period countdown and next premium date.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies/{{policyId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 32 — the premium ledger for this policy. Each period, and what happened.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies/{{policyId}}/premiums
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 33 — cancel inside the free-look window → a FULL premium refund.
#
# IRDAI requires a real insurer to honour this. Inside `freeLookEndsAt` the
# premium comes back over the M4 ledger on its own `PREMIUM_REFUND` leg; outside
# it, cover simply ends. The server decides which from the policy's dates — a
# refund flag on the request body would be a request for money the caller does
# not get to make.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/policies/{{policyId}}/cancel
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "Changed my mind after reading the exclusions"
}


########################################################################
# 40 · CLAIMS — OCR, the fraud gate, and the payout
########################################################################

### 40 — file a claim.
#
# Every guard runs BEFORE a row exists: the policy must be live, the incident
# must fall after the waiting period and inside the term, the amount must fit
# the REMAINING cover (an aggregate limit, not a per-claim one), and the
# `(policyId, dedupeHash)` unique index stops a duplicate that two concurrent
# submissions would otherwise both create.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "policyId": "{{policyId}}",
  "amountClaimedMinor": "40000",
  "incidentDate": "2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z",
  "description": "Fell at the site and could not work for eleven days. Treated at the district hospital."
}

### 41 — attach a CLEAN document.
#
# The dev OCR provider reads a JSON envelope so the pipeline is deterministic
# and CI-safe. A real vendor reads pixels; the CONTRACT — fields, amountMinor,
# tamperScore, confidence — is identical, which is what makes the swap one
# registry entry.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/documents
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----pravaah

------pravaah
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="docType"

PAYSLIP
------pravaah
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="document"; filename="payslip.json"
Content-Type: application/json

{ "amount": "400.00", "fields": { "employer": "Sunrise Constructions", "days_lost": "11" } }
------pravaah--

### 42 — THE SHOWPIECE: a FORGED document.
#
# `{"tamper": true}` scores 0.92 forgery likelihood. The document comes back
# `SUSPECT` and the claim can no longer auto-approve, however small and however
# otherwise-clean — the tamper score is a HARD GATE, not a weight that other
# favourable signals could outweigh. A forged document is not a probabilistic
# concern; it is evidence the claim is a fabrication.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/documents
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----pravaah

------pravaah
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="docType"

MEDICAL_BILL
------pravaah
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="document"; filename="bill.json"
Content-Type: application/json

{ "amount": "400.00", "tamper": true, "fields": { "hospital": "District Hospital" } }
------pravaah--

### 43 — a BLURRY document is not a forgery.
#
# `confidence` 0.4 → stays `UPLOADED` with a prompt to retake, rather than
# `SUSPECT`. Conflating the two would accuse somebody with a cracked phone
# camera of fraud — and that person is the one most likely to need the claim.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/documents
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=----pravaah

------pravaah
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="docType"

MEDICAL_BILL
------pravaah
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="document"; filename="bill.json"
Content-Type: application/json

{ "amount": "400.00", "blurry": true }
------pravaah--

### 44 — "I'm done uploading" → run the fraud gate.
#
# A separate call from §40 because documents arrive as separate multipart
# requests: scoring at submission would assess every claim as though it had
# none. This is where the M6 hook runs and where a small, clean, low-risk claim
# with verified documents auto-approves — settled in seconds, which for this
# claimant is the difference between the product working and being a form.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/finalise
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### 45 — the claim's status, with its documents and the payout reference.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 46 — my claims.
GET {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims?limit=25
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 47 — a DUPLICATE claim → 409.
# Same policy, same incident day, same amount → same `dedupeHash`. Re-send §40
# verbatim to see it.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "policyId": "{{policyId}}",
  "amountClaimedMinor": "40000",
  "incidentDate": "2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z",
  "description": "Fell at the site and could not work for eleven days. Treated at the district hospital."
}

### 48 — an incident INSIDE the waiting period → 400, with the reason.
# THE anti-adverse-selection control. Note it compares the INCIDENT date, never
# the filing date, so waiting to file does not convert an uncovered incident
# into a covered one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "policyId": "{{policyId}}",
  "amountClaimedMinor": "40000",
  "incidentDate": "2026-07-01T00:00:00.000Z",
  "description": "An incident that falls inside the policy's 30-day waiting period."
}

### 49 — a claim beyond the REMAINING cover → 400.
# An aggregate limit: two ₹8,000 claims against ₹10,000 of cover must not both
# pay in full.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "policyId": "{{policyId}}",
  "amountClaimedMinor": "99000000",
  "incidentDate": "2026-07-20T00:00:00.000Z",
  "description": "A claim for far more than this policy's remaining cover."
}

### 50 — withdraw a claim. Available while SUBMITTED or INFO_REQUESTED only.
# A decided claim is part of the book's loss history and cannot be retracted to
# tidy it up.
POST {{baseUrl}}/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/withdraw
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "reason": "Filed by mistake against the wrong policy"
}


########################################################################
# 60 · ADMIN — adjudication and the book
########################################################################

### 60 — the claim queue. OLDEST first, so the longest-waiting claimant is not
# starved by a newest-first ordering.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims?status=UNDER_REVIEW
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 61 — triage: only the claims the fraud hook flagged.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims?status=UNDER_REVIEW&flaggedOnly=true
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 62 — THE NUMBER AN INSURER JUDGE LOOKS FOR.
#
# Loss ratio = Σ claims paid ÷ Σ premiums earned, both read off the LEDGER
# rather than from `PremiumPayment.status` and `Claim.amountApprovedMinor`. The
# application tables record what we INTENDED; the ledger records what moved. So
# this figure reconciles against M4's trial balance (§90 of m4-payments.http) —
# two numbers that agree are what tell an insurer this is real.
#
# A loss ratio over 100% is REPORTED, not clamped, and the insurance float runs
# negative to match. An underwriting loss must be visible.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/book
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 63 — portfolio counts, the conversion funnel and the operational alarms.
# Watch `approvedUnpaidClaims`: an approved claim whose payout never settled is
# the quietest failure in the module — the claimant was told "approved", no
# error was logged, and the money never arrived.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/stats
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 64 — one claim, in full, for a reviewer.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 65 — APPROVE. `reason` is mandatory, including on an approval.
#
# An approval nobody has to justify is the shape of internal fraud. The acting
# admin comes from the authenticated session, never from the body.
#
# On approval the payout posts over the M4 ledger, idempotent on
# `claim-payout:{claimId}`. If it fails the claim stays APPROVED and RETRYABLE —
# there is deliberately no APPROVED → REJECTED edge, because once a claimant has
# been told their claim is approved it cannot be walked back for a payout error.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/decide
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "APPROVE",
  "reason": "Payslip and hospital record both consistent with eleven days of lost work."
}

### 66 — partially approve.
# Capped server-side at both the claimed amount and the remaining cover, so a
# fat-fingered extra zero cannot pay out more than the contract allows.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/decide
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "APPROVE",
  "approveMinor": "25000",
  "reason": "Documents support seven days of lost income rather than the eleven claimed."
}

### 67 — ask the claimant for more.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/decide
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "REQUEST_INFO",
  "reason": "Please upload the discharge summary — the bill alone does not show the dates of admission."
}

### 68 — reject, with a ground the claimant can act on.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/decide
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "REJECT",
  "reason": "The uploaded bill was assessed as altered; the amount does not match the hospital's own record."
}

### 69 — an amount on a REJECT → 400. A contradicted intent about somebody's
# money is refused rather than half-honoured.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/claims/{{claimId}}/decide
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "decision": "REJECT",
  "approveMinor": "25000",
  "reason": "Contradictory: rejecting while naming an approved amount."
}

### 70 — reveal one document's OCR extraction.
#
# Field-encrypted at rest, because a hospital bill names a diagnosis and a
# treating physician — health data about an identified person. Decrypting it
# writes its OWN audit row naming the admin: an admin who reads a claimant's
# medical extraction has done something that must be attributable.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/documents/{{docId}}/reveal
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}

### 71 — the policy estate.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/policies?status=ACTIVE
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 72 — the premium history for one policy, for a support call.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/policies/{{policyId}}/premiums
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 73 — add a product. No deploy needed to launch a cover.
#
# The refinements refuse a product that would be unsafe to sell: a waiting
# period longer than the term could never pay a claim, and a zero waiting period
# on income protection makes buy-after-the-incident rational.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/products
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "type": "INCOME_PROTECTION",
  "name": "Income Protection — Monsoon Cover",
  "tagline": "For the weeks when the site floods and there is no work to go to.",
  "currency": "INR",
  "baseRateBps": 1100,
  "minCoverMinor": "500000",
  "maxCoverMinor": "3000000",
  "termDays": 180,
  "waitingPeriodDays": 21,
  "freeLookDays": 15,
  "exclusions": [
    "Loss of income from voluntarily leaving a job.",
    "Days you were able to work but chose not to.",
    "Any incident inside your 21-day waiting period."
  ],
  "disclaimer": "Pravaah provides information, not medical or financial advice. Cover, exclusions and claim decisions are governed by the insurer's policy wording."
}

### 74 — a waiting period longer than the term → 400.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/products
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "type": "HEALTH_TOPUP",
  "name": "Unpayable Cover",
  "currency": "INR",
  "baseRateBps": 400,
  "minCoverMinor": "100000",
  "maxCoverMinor": "500000",
  "termDays": 30,
  "waitingPeriodDays": 60,
  "exclusions": ["Everything, effectively."],
  "disclaimer": "Pravaah provides information, not medical or financial advice."
}

### 75 — retire a product without deleting its history.
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/products/{{incomeProtectionId}}/active
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isActive": false
}

### 76 — force every lifecycle sweep now: expire quotes, run premium dunning
# (ACTIVE → GRACE → LAPSED), expire terms, resume stuck money, retry unpaid
# payouts, close abandoned info requests.
POST {{baseUrl}}/admin/insurance/run-lifecycle
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{}


########################################################################
# 80 · THE INSURER'S CALLBACKS (mock, signed for real)
########################################################################

### 80 — what the module is actually running.
#
# Surfaced through the API on purpose. A judge or an auditor should be able to
# read which insurer bound a policy and which engine read a claim document
# without inferring it from behaviour — and a build that quietly presented a
# mock as a licensed insurer would be the one thing that discredits everything
# else here.
GET {{baseUrl}}/mock/insurer/sign
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 81 — sign a callback body without sending it, so it can be replayed by hand.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mock/insurer/sign
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "eventId": "evt-mock-001",
  "insurerClaimRef": "MOCK-CLM-ABCDEF0123",
  "status": "APPROVED",
  "approvedMinor": "40000"
}

### 82 — fire a correctly signed callback at ourselves.
#
# Exercises the REAL webhook path: HMAC over the raw bytes, timing-safe compare,
# `WebhookEvent` replay dedupe, then the claims state machine. The mock's
# verification is genuine rather than a stub returning `true` — a mock that
# waved signatures through would mean the production path had never once run.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mock/insurer/fire-webhook
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "eventId": "evt-mock-002",
  "insurerClaimRef": "MOCK-CLM-ABCDEF0123",
  "status": "IN_ASSESSMENT"
}

### 83 — replay it. Deduped on `(provider, eventId)` → `{ duplicate: true }`.
POST {{baseUrl}}/mock/insurer/fire-webhook
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "eventId": "evt-mock-002",
  "insurerClaimRef": "MOCK-CLM-ABCDEF0123",
  "status": "IN_ASSESSMENT"
}

### 84 — an UNSIGNED callback → 403, and nothing recorded.
# An unsigned callback tells us nothing about whether a claim decision is real,
# so it must not be recorded as though it did. The response is deliberately
# uninformative: an endpoint that reported whether a claim reference was
# recognised would be an oracle for probing which references exist.
POST {{baseUrl}}/webhooks/insurance/mock-insurer
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "eventId": "evt-forged-001",
  "insurerClaimRef": "MOCK-CLM-ABCDEF0123",
  "status": "APPROVED"
}


########################################################################
# 90 · THE PROOF
########################################################################

### 90 — the trial balance (M4). Must be EXACTLY zero, in every currency.
#
# Run this AFTER a premium and a claim payout. M5 posts through the same
# `LedgerService`, so a premium or a payout that did not conserve money would
# show up here — which is what makes the loss ratio in §62 a figure that can be
# trusted rather than asserted.
GET {{baseUrl}}/admin/payments/trial-balance
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 91 — the wallet statement. The premium and the payout appear as ledger
# lines on their own legs (PREMIUM, CLAIM_PAYOUT), beside the M4 transfers.
GET {{baseUrl}}/payments/wallet/statement
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}
